# scry's default rule pack (S4)
#
# The canonical text is mirrored by `Scry.demoPackText` in
# `lib/Scry/RuleExamples.wiz`, which the `scry-taint` tests parse. The format is
# implemented by `Scry.parseRulePack` (`lib/Scry/Rules.wiz`):
#
#   rule ID
#     kind taint | forbidden | memory
#     severity error | warning | note
#     cwe CWE-NN                    (metadata; emitted in SARIF properties.cwe)
#     message TEXT
#     source NAME result | arg K
#     sanitise NAME result | arg K
#     sink NAME arg K
#     call NAME                     (forbidden rules)
#     check NAME                    (memory rules: use-after-free)
#   end
#
# Names match with `*` as a wildcard over the whole callee name. A `kind memory` rule
# promotes a points-to warning to a finding; that finding is `unchecked` (its evidence
# is the memory model, not a source -> sink derivation).
#
# The CWE tags are the *targeted* weakness, not a claim that a finding proves the
# weakness. A taint rule says "attacker-controlled data reaches this argument"; whether
# that is exploitable still depends on the callee and the surrounding checks.
#
# source/sink/sanitiser table (also in docs/libraries.md):
#
#   rule              sources                                  sanitisers
#   command-injection getenv,recv,read[1],fgets[0],getline[0]   escape_shell,shellescape,
#                     fread[0]                                 sanitize[0],quote_arg
#   format-string     getenv,recv,read[1],fgets[0]              sanitize_format
#   buffer-overflow   getenv,recv,read[1],fgets[0]              clamp_len,checked_size
#   integer-overflow  getenv,recv,read[1],fgets[0]              checked_size,clamp_size
#   path-traversal    getenv,recv,read[1],fgets[0]              sanitize_path,confine_path
#
# `strcpy-overflow` and `gets-overflow` are forbidden calls: unsafe regardless of taint.
#
# The C lowering folds `malloc`/`calloc` into `Wil.alloc` and leaves `realloc` havoc, so
# the `integer-overflow` size sinks for those names are a documented gap in this C
# slice; `alloca` stays a call and is the positive control. The pack keeps the names so
# another frontend (or a later `alloc`-size model) can use the same rule.

rule command-injection
  kind taint
  severity error
  cwe CWE-78
  message Attacker-controlled data reaches a command interpreter
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  source getline arg 0
  source fread arg 0
  sanitise escape_shell result
  sanitise shellescape result
  sanitise sanitize arg 0
  sanitise quote_arg result
  sink system arg 0
  sink popen arg 0
  sink exec* arg 0
  sink posix_spawn arg 1
  sink posix_spawnp arg 1
end

rule format-string
  kind taint
  severity warning
  cwe CWE-134
  message Attacker-controlled data is used as a format string
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  sanitise sanitize_format result
  sink printf arg 0
  sink fprintf arg 1
  sink sprintf arg 1
  sink snprintf arg 2
  sink vprintf arg 0
  sink vfprintf arg 1
  sink vsprintf arg 1
  sink vsnprintf arg 2
  sink syslog arg 1
  sink dprintf arg 1
  sink asprintf arg 1
end

rule buffer-overflow
  kind taint
  severity warning
  cwe CWE-787
  message Attacker-controlled length reaches a copy or read; the destination can overflow
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  sanitise clamp_len result
  sanitise checked_size result
  sink memcpy arg 2
  sink memmove arg 2
  sink memset arg 2
  sink read arg 2
  sink recv arg 2
  sink snprintf arg 1
end

rule gets-overflow
  kind forbidden
  severity error
  cwe CWE-242
  message gets cannot bound its write; use fgets with a size
  call gets
end

rule strcpy-overflow
  kind forbidden
  severity warning
  cwe CWE-120
  message the copy does not bound its destination; use a size-checked variant
  call strcpy
  call strcat
  call sprintf
  call vsprintf
  call stpcpy
end

rule integer-overflow
  kind taint
  severity warning
  cwe CWE-190
  message Attacker-controlled value reaches an allocation size; the size may overflow or exhaust memory
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  sanitise checked_size result
  sanitise clamp_size result
  sink malloc arg 0
  sink calloc arg 0
  sink calloc arg 1
  sink realloc arg 1
  sink alloca arg 0
end

rule path-traversal
  kind taint
  severity error
  cwe CWE-22
  message Attacker-controlled data reaches a filesystem path
  source getenv result
  source recv result
  source read arg 1
  source fgets arg 0
  sanitise sanitize_path result
  sanitise confine_path result
  sink fopen arg 0
  sink freopen arg 0
  sink open arg 0
  sink openat arg 1
  sink creat arg 0
  sink stat arg 0
  sink lstat arg 0
  sink unlink arg 0
  sink remove arg 0
  sink rename arg 0
  sink rename arg 1
  sink chdir arg 0
  sink opendir arg 0
  sink access arg 0
  sink mkdir arg 0
end

rule use-after-free
  kind memory
  severity error
  cwe CWE-416
  message A memory location is accessed after a free on some path
  check use-after-free
end
